<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tres Zapotes</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 300 CE (approx.) · capital Tres Zapotes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A long-lived Olmec and Epi-Olmec centre in the Tuxtla region, whose Stela C bears one of the oldest Long Count calendar dates in the Americas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tres Zapotes grew on the western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a> in southern Veracruz around 900 BCE, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec</a> period. Unlike <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/san-lorenzo">San Lorenzo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/la-venta">La Venta</a>, it survived the wider Olmec decline after 400 BCE and continued as a regional centre for centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The site has two colossal heads from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec</a> phase. In the following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture">Epi-Olmec</a> period, power seems to have been shared among several groups whose plazas were spread across the settlement rather than held by a single ruler. Stela C carries a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-long-count-calendar">Long Count</a> date equivalent to 32 BCE, and the site illuminates the transition from Olmec to later writing and calendar traditions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tres Zapotes declined by about 300 CE, though the area remained inhabited. Its inscriptions are key evidence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-long-count-calendar">Long Count</a> and early writing developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> before their flowering among the Maya.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tres-zapotes.jpg" alt="Tres Zapotes" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Heartland_Overview_4.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tres Zapotes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Olmec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Mixe-Zoquean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mexico</dd></dl></aside></div>

